---
import Example from '../library/Example.astro';
import { withBase } from '../design/meta';
import Prose from '../components/fundamentals/Prose.astro';
---

<Example title="Rich text" layout="block" description="What Markdown produces, set as a magazine sets it: a drop cap (on a paragraph marked data-dropcap), headings, a subheading (a paragraph marked data-subheading, right under its heading), lists, a quotation, a dinkus.">
  <Prose>
    <p data-dropcap>Walking is the whole interface. The planet turns under your feet, so the path ahead always rises to meet you, and every landmark you reach opens a part of the story. The <a href={withBase('/')}>classic site</a> has the same words as plain pages.</p>
    <h2>What’s on the planet</h2>
    <p data-subheading>A small world with a story at every landmark</p>
    <p>Seven landmarks, a plaza at the pole, a family at home and a small dog called Chopper. Each landmark is a section of the site:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The workshop</strong> holds the case studies.</li>
      <li><strong>The town hall</strong> is about leading teams.</li>
      <li><strong>The library</strong> holds the writing.</li>
    </ul>
    <blockquote><p>The classic site must stay reachable from every state.</p></blockquote>
    <hr />
    <h3>How it’s drawn</h3>
    <p>Everything is built in code from small parts, painted with generated textures, and lit by a sun that sets. Read the <code>art-pipeline</code> notes for how.</p>
  </Prose>
</Example>

<Example title="Section marks" layout="block" description="A heading 2 with its mark (data-heading-mark, part of its name): hung in the margin beside it where there's room (72rem and wider), its top on the heading's as the library's tiers are, and over it on a narrower screen. A section marker (a paragraph marked data-section-mark) sets the same big marigold type in the column, for a number or Chapter 1.">
  <Prose breakout>
    <h2 data-marked><span data-heading-mark>1 </span>Learning the medium</h2>
    <p data-subheading>Chapter 1 · May – Jul 2024</p>
    <p>Before personalization was anyone’s job, I spent time learning what high-quality image generation takes.</p>
    <p data-section-mark>Chapter 2</p>
    <h2>The demand surfaces</h2>
    <p>Through the year, personalization moved from a side interest to an explicit priority.</p>
  </Prose>
</Example>

<Example title="Lead size" layout="block" description="A short introduction, a size up.">
  <Prose size="lead">
    <p>A portfolio you can walk around, and the same portfolio as plain pages.</p>
  </Prose>
</Example>

<Example title="Compact, with nested lists" layout="block" description="A card's words, as a collection item sets them: the interface's size at the reading line height, paragraphs and lists a little apart, a list nested under an item, struck-through words.">
  <Prose size="compact">
    <p>Each stage answered the same question in its own way, and <del>none of them</del> each left something behind.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Records</strong>: a contact list shared by programs
        <ul>
          <li>Useful plumbing</li>
          <li>No experience of its own</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Hubs</strong>: people as a feed</li>
    </ul>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the person</li>
      <li>Act on them where you are</li>
    </ol>
  </Prose>
</Example>
